const definitions = `
Covenant|A covenant is a binding relationship with promises and obligations. The word itself does not occur in 2 Samuel 7:8–16, but Psalm 89:3–4 describes God's commitment to David in covenant terms. The royal promise includes succession, discipline and continuing love; it is not approval of everything a king might do. Mary's remembrance of Abraham in Luke 1:54–55 belongs to another part of this larger story of commitment. Compare the promises and recipients before treating all covenant passages as interchangeable.
House|A house can mean a physical building, the people of a household or a line of descendants. In 2 Samuel 7, David's desire for a temple meets God's promise of a continuing royal family. The shifts in meaning are part of the passage's argument. Luke 1:27 and 1:33 use family and people language when describing David's house and Jacob's house. The shared vocabulary helps connect the accounts, but the sentence determines which meaning is present.
David|David is the ruler addressed in 2 Samuel 7 and the ancestor whose family becomes central to the royal promise. His death is anticipated, so the future cannot rest on his personal lifespan. Luke identifies Joseph with David's family and Bethlehem with David's city, while Gabriel speaks of David's throne. These are distinct connections of ancestry, place and authority. They invite readers to watch how the promised future takes shape without assuming that Jesus simply repeats every feature of David's reign.
Throne|A throne is a ruler's seat and, by extension, the authority of that ruler and the continuity of a dynasty. In 2 Samuel 7, the promise concerns rule after David's death, not the survival of a particular chair. The surrounding father–son language also includes accountability for the successor's wrongdoing. Luke 1:32–33 returns to the throne while describing Mary's son. Reading both settings makes the continuity visible while leaving room to consider what is distinctive about the later announcement.
Faithfulness|Faithfulness means reliability in a relationship or commitment. In David's story, God's continuing love is distinguished from the discipline of a king who does wrong. That distinction prevents faithfulness from becoming a promise that no painful consequences can follow. Luke's opening chapters often remember earlier words while describing events now unfolding. The reader can ask how reliability is shown across changing generations, rather than measuring it only by an immediate outcome or the success of one human leader.
Yahweh|Yahweh is the personal name of Israel's God as printed in the WEB Old Testament. Many English editions use LORD in capitals at these places. In the Davidic promise, this is the God who recalls bringing Israel from Egypt and taking David from the flock. The name therefore belongs to a remembered relationship and history. The New Testament title 'Lord' must still be read in its own sentence; an English title alone cannot settle whom every occurrence identifies.
King|A king rules a people, but these passages ask what kind of rule can answer their distress. Isaiah 9 places the promised child against oppression, warfare and the hope of justice. Royal titles there describe responsibility and character as well as power. Gabriel's words in Luke 1:32–33 connect the coming child with David's throne. Compare the ruler's relationship to his people and the source of his authority before assuming that familiar models of political success explain the promised kingship.
Justice|Justice concerns what is right in judgments and in the ordering of a community. Isaiah's royal hope follows images of oppressive burdens and battle; justice gives the promised peace its moral shape. It is not simply whatever benefits the victorious group. As Luke's story develops, the treatment of vulnerable people will give further substance to questions of right rule. Begin with the concrete wrongs and needs named in this passage, allowing discussion to connect them with present responsibilities without equating different historical situations.
Righteousness|Righteousness describes what is right in relation to God and others, but its emphasis varies with the sentence. Isaiah 9 links it to the establishment of a just kingdom. Luke 1:6 uses it to describe Zacharias and Elizabeth's life of obedience, even while they experience painful childlessness. The word does not make their circumstances evidence of either reward or rejection. Pay attention to whether the text is describing a ruler's order, a person's conduct or another relationship before applying one definition everywhere.
Peace|Peace can include safety, well-being and rightly ordered relationships, not only the absence of fighting. Isaiah connects it with the end of oppression and enduring justice; Micah locates it in the person and care of the coming ruler. In Luke 2 it belongs to the announcement of the child's birth. Those settings overlap without saying everything in the same way. The word opens a question about what people actually need for life together, rather than promising every reader an immediate feeling of calm.
Light|Light is an image whose meaning comes from the darkness it answers. Isaiah 9 places it amid a people's distress; Isaiah 49 links it to salvation reaching other nations. Simeon's words in Luke 2:32 bring revelation and the peoples together. Acts 13:47 will return to the servant passage in a new missionary setting. These are textual connections to explore, not a reason to make every lamp or bright appearance in Scripture mean the same thing.
Hope|Hope is expectation directed towards a future that is not yet visible. The prophets give that expectation concrete content: secure life, just rule, gathering and rescue. It does not erase their descriptions of siege, exhaustion or apparent failure. Luke introduces people whose waiting has taken different forms over many years. Rather than treating hope as positive thinking, follow what has been promised, who is waiting and what might count as fulfilment within each scene.
Bethlehem|Bethlehem is associated with David's family story and is named in Micah as small among Judah's clans. Its significance in that prophecy stands against a scene of siege and a humiliated ruler. Luke 2 identifies it as David's city and explains Joseph's journey through his ancestry. The connection is concrete, but the texts do not describe every detail of the birth place. Distinguish their references to town, family and promise from the architectural scenery familiar from later Christmas traditions.
Shepherd|A shepherd tends and protects a flock. Micah uses that work as an image of the coming ruler's care, sustained by God's strength. In Luke 2 the shepherds are actual workers watching their sheep, receiving and passing on news. The same vocabulary does not assign them the ruler's role. Neither does Luke state that all shepherds had one moral reputation or social standing. Read the occupation and the leadership image in their own settings before drawing a connection between them.
Sovereignty|Sovereignty describes supreme authority. In Micah, the coming ruler's strength belongs to Yahweh, while the visible ruler at the opening is vulnerable to humiliation. That contrast helps locate the source of lasting security. The term is a study concept, not a word counted in the passage. It should not be used to bypass the text's account of distress or the responsibility of human rulers. Explore how God's authority and the people's waiting are held together in this particular promise.
Servant|A servant acts in relation to another's authority or purpose. Isaiah 49 gives this role a demanding shape: calling, preparation, apparent failure, Israel's restoration and a wider mission. The servant is called Israel yet also sent to restore Israel, a feature that deserves discussion. In Mary's song, Israel is God's servant; elsewhere Mary calls herself his servant. These are related expressions of belonging and vocation, but each has a different speaker and setting. Shared wording should encourage comparison rather than erase those differences.
Nations|Nations refers to peoples or communities beyond one local group; in many biblical contexts the focus is on peoples other than Israel. Isaiah 49 addresses distant listeners before describing a mission that includes Israel's restoration and extends to the earth's ends. The wider purpose does not simply cancel the earlier one. Luke 2:32 will name Gentiles alongside Israel, offering a further point of comparison. Let the widening audience emerge from these passages rather than reading the whole later journey into every mention of a nation.
Salvation|Salvation is rescue or deliverance, and the surrounding passage shows what people need rescue from or restoration to. Isaiah 49 joins the gathering of Israel to a purpose reaching the nations. Simeon recognises salvation in the child Jesus, while Luke 1:77 connects knowledge of salvation with forgiveness of sins. These connections enrich the term without reducing it to only political relief or only an inward experience. Follow the agents, people and needs named in each scene as its meaning develops.
Prayer|Prayer is address to God in dependence, worship or petition. In Luke 1, the gathered people pray outside while Zacharias offers incense inside; a personal request is answered within that shared act of worship. Luke does not give us the crowd's exact words. The scene places prayer within ordinary religious practice before an unexpected announcement. Later Jesus also prays at his baptism. These settings invite attention to relationship and trust without turning the particular sequence of events into a formula for guaranteed outcomes.
Holy Spirit|God's Spirit acts throughout Luke's opening scenes: filling, guiding, revealing and identifying the promised Son. These are particular actions with particular people, not interchangeable descriptions of one experience. In Luke 1:47 Mary's spirit means her inner life, whereas the Holy Spirit in 1:41 identifies God's presence and action. Acts will continue this theme, but first trace what the Spirit actually does in the selected passage. This keeps the connection grounded and avoids adding details from another scene too quickly.
Repentance|Repentance is a turning towards God that involves a changed direction of life. John's preparation of a people in Luke 1 becomes explicit preaching in Luke 3. His hearers ask what to do, and he names generosity, honesty and refusal of extortion. These are concrete fruits of the response, not identical occupational instructions for every person. Ancestry cannot replace this turning. The passage allows discussion of both the inward change and its outward evidence without treating outward performance as a price paid for forgiveness.
Faith|Faith involves trust in a person or word considered reliable. Luke sets Zacharias's request for assurance beside the announcement's promised fulfilment, and Elizabeth later blesses Mary for believing the Lord's words. The scenes include questions, fear and incomplete understanding; they do not define trust as possessing every answer in advance. Attend to the particular word being received and the response it produces. This is more precise than calling every strong expectation faith, regardless of what it rests upon.
Promise|A promise commits a speaker to a future act. In these readings the announcement often precedes any visible sign of fulfilment and may reach beyond the recipient's lifetime. The promise to Zacharias gives a name and a calling, not only the birth of a child. Luke repeatedly connects words with events while also recording periods of waiting. Identify exactly what is promised before extending it into a guarantee about circumstances the text does not address.
Grace|Grace names freely given favour or kindness. Gabriel tells Mary that she has found favour with God before the announced child is conceived. The scene foregrounds God's initiative while giving Mary's question and willing response real importance. Favour does not imply that the calling will bring social ease or remove sorrow; later scenes will complicate such an expectation. Begin with what is given in this announcement and how it is received, rather than making the word a vague label for anything pleasant.
Son of God|Sonship is relationship language whose biblical uses need their own context. The promise to David describes a father–son relationship with his royal successor, including discipline for wrongdoing. Luke 1:35 connects Jesus' title with the Spirit's action in his conception, while Luke 3:22 addresses him as the beloved Son. These passages belong in conversation but do not make every detail identical. Follow how Luke identifies this particular Son before importing all other uses of the title into the scene.
Kingdom|A kingdom is a king's rule together with the people and realm under that authority. Gabriel's announcement joins Jesus' reign over Jacob's house to David's throne and says it will have no end. The vocabulary recalls a family promise while opening questions about the character of this rule. Luke will explore the kingdom through the life and teaching of Jesus. For now, notice who grants authority, whom the reign concerns and how its duration contrasts with ordinary human succession.
Incarnation|Incarnation is a later theological term for the Son of God becoming truly human. It is a study heading rather than a word found in this passage. Luke's announcement speaks of conception, birth and a child whose identity is explained through God's action. The following scenes describe real infancy and growth. The term helps gather those observations, but it should not replace them or encourage imaginary biological explanations that the account does not provide.
Obedience|Obedience is a response expressed in action towards an authority or word. Mary's willing reply, the parents' observance of the law and Jesus' return to live under their care are distinct examples in these readings. They are not the same action or circumstance. Luke can describe obedience alongside incomplete understanding, so the word does not imply that the participants already grasp the whole story. Ask which command, responsibility or relationship is being honoured in the selected scene.
Mercy|Mercy is compassionate care towards those in need, often expressed through faithful action. Mary's song joins help for the lowly and hungry with remembered commitment to Abraham and his descendants. It therefore has both a human and a historical setting. The language of remembering does not imply that God had lost information; it points to action consistent with his earlier word. Read the song's contrasts closely without making them a mechanical explanation of every person's wealth or hardship.
Humble|Humble or lowly can describe a person's unimportant or vulnerable position as well as an attitude. Mary's lowly condition is set beside rulers, the proud, the hungry and the rich. Begin with these social contrasts before reducing the word to a private feeling about oneself. The song celebrates God's regard for those easily overlooked. It leaves room to consider both circumstances and posture before God, without declaring every disadvantaged person virtuous or every influential person identical.
Holiness|Holiness concerns God's distinct identity and what is set apart in relation to him. Mary praises God's holy name; other readings describe the Holy Spirit, a holy child and consecration under the law. These uses are related but not interchangeable. In the song, holiness appears alongside might and mercy, giving shape to the character of the one being praised. It should not be reduced to social distance, ritual alone or an automatic judgment about how respectable someone appears.
Humanity of Christ|This phrase gathers Luke's concrete descriptions of Jesus' birth, dependence, family life and growth. A baby is wrapped and laid down; a child listens, asks questions and increases in wisdom and stature. These observations give his human life substance. They should not be flattened into an appearance of childhood or expanded into invented stories about the unrecorded years. Luke also makes distinctive claims about his identity, so discussion can hold real human development together with the narrative's testimony about the Son.
Providence|Providence describes God's care and purpose working through the events of life. Luke 2 places an imperial registration, a family's travel and a birth in one account, following the earlier royal announcement. The narrative invites attention to how these events belong together, but does not explain every decision by every official. The concept should help us read this particular story carefully, not license a confident explanation of every political event or hardship as if its whole purpose were known.
Humility|Humility involves accepting a lowly place without making status the measure of worth. In the birth scene, the child announced with royal titles arrives amid ordinary dependence and limited accommodation. Luke gives concrete details rather than an abstract lesson in self-esteem. The contrast between identity and circumstances can shape reflection, while leaving room to distinguish voluntary humility from hardship imposed on others. Poverty or vulnerability should not simply be romanticised or treated as proof of superior character.
Saviour|A saviour is one who rescues. In Luke 2:11 the title identifies the newborn child before any public act of rescue is narrated. Its placement beside Christ and Lord gives the announcement weight, while the sign points to a baby in a feeding trough. The earlier promise of salvation and the later unfolding story help fill out what the rescue involves. Let that meaning develop through the narrative rather than expecting one title to explain the whole mission at once.
Christ|Christ means Anointed One and corresponds to the title Messiah; it is not a surname. In Luke's birth announcement it identifies the child in relation to Israel's expectation of God's appointed deliverer. Earlier royal promises help form that expectation, but the baby's circumstances are not those of an obvious enthronement. The title therefore opens a question about how his calling will be recognised. Follow the narrative's account of his identity and work rather than supplying a complete political programme from the title alone.
Lord|Lord is a title of authority whose referent depends on the sentence. In Luke 2, the Lord's angel brings news, and the child is announced as Christ the Lord. The repetition draws attention to authority and identity, but careful reading still distinguishes the speakers, subjects and relationships. The WEB's Old Testament use of Yahweh is another translation convention. Do not assume every printed occurrence of Lord refers to the same person or carries exactly the same emphasis.
Glory|Glory concerns honour and manifested greatness. In the shepherds' scene, the Lord's glory shines around them, the heavenly company gives glory to God, and the shepherds return glorifying him. Visible brightness and spoken praise are connected but different expressions. The humble sign of the child stands alongside the heavenly splendour. Read that contrast before treating glory as worldly prestige or assuming every later use of the word describes visible light.
Witness|A witness tells what has been seen, heard or known. Luke 2 does not give the shepherds the technical title of apostles, but it does describe them making the message known after their encounter. Their report is tied to words received and an event seen. This pattern offers a modest preparation for testimony in Luke–Acts. It does not make every later role identical; the basis and responsibility of each witness must be followed in its own scene.
Redemption|Redemption is release or rescue, language that can evoke liberation from bondage and the recovery of a people. Anna speaks to those awaiting Jerusalem's redemption, locating the hope within Israel's worship and communal longing. The scene does not list every expectation held by those hearers. Luke's later story will develop the manner of God's rescue. For now, distinguish the hope expressed by the waiting people from details the reader may already know from later chapters.
Gentiles|Gentiles refers to peoples other than the Jewish people in this setting. Simeon places revelation for the Gentiles alongside glory for Israel, echoing the wider horizon of Isaiah's servant passage. The pairing does not require erasing Israel's place in the promise. It allows readers to consider a salvation whose audience is wider than one people. Luke–Acts will return to that question; the temple scene introduces the horizon without narrating all the steps by which it will be explored.
Prophecy|Prophecy is speech that communicates a message understood as coming from God, not merely a prediction of dates. Anna is called a prophetess, and Simeon speaks both of salvation and of opposition to the child. Their words interpret the present encounter as well as anticipate what lies ahead. Notice the connection between worship, recognition and warning. Prophetic language should be read in its literary setting rather than treated as a collection of isolated clues about future events.
Father|Father may name a parent, an ancestor or a relationship to God. Luke 2:48–49 places Mary's reference to Jesus' father beside Jesus' own reference to his Father, making the shift significant. His subsequent return in obedience prevents the divine relationship from cancelling ordinary family life. Elsewhere David is an ancestor in royal language. Reading the surrounding speech helps distinguish these uses and allows Jesus' distinctive identity to emerge without forcing one meaning onto every occurrence.
Scripture|Scripture refers to the sacred writings received as authoritative within the community. At Passover, Jesus is found listening and questioning among teachers in the temple, within Israel's life of worship and instruction. Luke does not supply the subject or text of every exchange. That restraint matters: the scene invites attentive learning without authorising us to invent the discussion. As this study moves through earlier promises and Luke's narrative, comparison should remain rooted in what the passages actually say.
Baptism|Baptism involves washing or immersion with water; Luke 3 places John's baptism within a summons to repentance for forgiveness. John distinguishes his work from what the coming one will do with the Holy Spirit and fire. Jesus is then baptised, and the narrative records prayer, the Spirit and the heavenly voice. These events are connected without being identical accounts of each participant's inner state. The passage should lead the comparison before later debates are made to determine every detail.
Forgiveness|Forgiveness is the remission of sins, a restored relation rather than the denial that wrong has occurred. John announces repentance and baptism in relation to forgiveness, and his hearers are directed towards concrete changes in how they treat others. Those changes are described as fitting fruit, not a fee paid to obtain mercy. Luke–Acts will return to forgiveness repeatedly. Begin here with the relationship between the announcement, the response and the wrongs named in John's answers.
Sin|Sin is wrongdoing before God, which this passage makes visible through failures of generosity, dishonest collection, extortion and abuses of power. John's warning reaches ordinary hearers and rulers alike. Family ancestry and public position do not remove accountability. The scene gives substance to repentance without reducing all wrongdoing to one social category. Its practical examples can open discussion about responsibility today, while preserving the historical differences between our circumstances and the people John addresses.
`;
export const glossaryDefinitions=Object.fromEntries(definitions.trim().split('\n').map(line=>line.split('|')));
export function glossaryForWeek(entries, session) {
  return weeklyGlossaryTerms[session.number].map(word=>{
    const entry=entries.find(g=>g.word===word);
    if(!entry || !glossaryDefinitions[word]) throw new Error(`Missing weekly glossary term: ${word}`);
    return {...entry,definition:glossaryDefinitions[word].replace(/((?:[^.!?]+[.!?] ){2})/, (first)=>first.trim()+'\n\n'),reference:session.passage,week:session.number};
  });
}
